I don&#39;t agree with this part. People of intense faith are often even more susceptible to doubt. I&#39;m more likely to believe that Fanatic&#39;s bravado is a defense mechanism. She wants to believe that she&#39;s doing the right thing and on the right path, but isn&#39;t always sure. It is not uncommon for people who are insecure to try and hide their insecurities with false bravado.My theory on why Apostate was able to mess with Fanatic&#39;s head was because he could see through all that. He could see Fanatic&#39;s insecurities and played on those in a way no one ever had. Whereas most of the people around Fanatic weren&#39;t able to see through her fascade, Apostate could.My personal theory is that Prime Wardens Fanatic represents Fanatic coming to terms with herself and realizing that she can trust people and show them who she really is.

[/quote]

Christopher told me at Kantcon a few years ago that Fanatic puffs up and uses a lot of bravado about her faith with people in public but in private suffers from intense doubt and grief over that doubt.&nbsp; I think you&#39;re definitely on the right path.<br /><br />Fanatic is no straw-man religious zealot.&nbsp; She&#39;s a fully realized, three-dimensional character with a faith beyond mere fanaticism.
